Task 1: Set Up Traffic Routing Based on HTTP Headers
Subtask 1.1: Deploy Sample Applications
First, we'll deploy multiple versions of a sample application to demonstrate header-based routing.

Create the namespace and enable Istio injection:
kubectl create namespace traffic-routing
kubectl label namespace traffic-routing istio-injection=enabled
Deploy the first version of the application:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-reviews-v1
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: reviews
      version: v1
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: reviews
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: reviews
        image: docker.io/istio/examples-bookinfo-reviews-v1:1.16.2
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9080
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-reviews-v2
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: reviews
      version: v2
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: reviews
        version: v2
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: reviews
        image: docker.io/istio/examples-bookinfo-reviews-v2:1.16.2
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9080
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: reviews
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 9080
    name: http
  selector:
    app: reviews
EOF
Deploy a client application for testing:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: sleep
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: sleep
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: sleep
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: sleep
        image: curlimages/curl
        command: ["/bin/sleep", "3650d"]
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
EOF
Verify all pods are running:
kubectl get pods -n traffic-routing
Wait until all pods show Running status before proceeding.

Subtask 1.2: Create Basic Destination Rule
Define destination rules to identify different versions of the service.

Create the destination rule:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews-destination
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  host: reviews
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
EOF
Verify the destination rule is created:
kubectl get destinationrule -n traffic-routing
Subtask 1.3: Configure Header-Based Routing
Now we'll create virtual services to route traffic based on HTTP headers.

Create a virtual service for header-based routing:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-header-routing
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        user-type:
          exact: premium
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
EOF
This configuration routes requests with the header user-type: premium to version 2, while all other requests go to version 1.

Verify the virtual service is created:
kubectl get virtualservice -n traffic-routing
Subtask 1.4: Test Header-Based Routing
Test the routing configuration to ensure it works correctly.

Get the sleep pod name:
SLEEP_POD=$(kubectl get pod -l app=sleep -n traffic-routing -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
echo "Sleep pod: $SLEEP_POD"
Test default routing (should go to v1):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Look for the response - version 1 typically shows reviews without star ratings.

Test premium user routing (should go to v2):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s -H "user-type: premium" http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Version 2 typically shows reviews with black star ratings.

Run multiple tests to verify consistency:
for i in {1..5}; do
  echo "Test $i - Default routing:"
  kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s http://reviews:9080/reviews/1 | grep -o "reviews-v[12]" || echo "No version info found"
  
  echo "Test $i - Premium routing:"
  kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s -H "user-type: premium" http://reviews:9080/reviews/1 | grep -o "reviews-v[12]" || echo "No version info found"
  echo "---"
done
Task 2: Define Subsets for Version-Based Routing
Subtask 2.1: Deploy Additional Service Versions
Deploy a third version of the service to demonstrate more complex routing scenarios.

Deploy version 3 of the reviews service:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: bookinfo-reviews-v3
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: reviews
      version: v3
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: reviews
        version: v3
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: reviews
        image: docker.io/istio/examples-bookinfo-reviews-v3:1.16.2
        imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
        ports:
        - containerPort: 9080
EOF
Wait for the new pod to be ready:
kubectl get pods -n traffic-routing -w
Press Ctrl+C when all pods are running.

Subtask 2.2: Update Destination Rule with New Subset
Update the destination rule to include the new version.

Update the destination rule:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: reviews-destination
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  host: reviews
  subsets:
  - name: v1
    labels:
      version: v1
  - name: v2
    labels:
      version: v2
  - name: v3
    labels:
      version: v3
EOF
Verify the updated destination rule:
kubectl describe destinationrule reviews-destination -n traffic-routing
Subtask 2.3: Implement Advanced Routing Rules
Create more sophisticated routing rules using multiple criteria.

Create an advanced virtual service:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-advanced-routing
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        user-type:
          exact: premium
        user-agent:
          regex: ".*Chrome.*"
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v3
  - match:
    - headers:
        user-type:
          exact: premium
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
  - match:
    - headers:
        test-user:
          exact: "true"
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v3
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
EOF
This configuration implements the following routing logic:

Premium users with Chrome browser → v3
Other premium users → v2
Test users → v3
All other users → v1
Verify the virtual service:
kubectl get virtualservice reviews-advanced-routing -n traffic-routing -o yaml
Subtask 2.4: Test Advanced Routing Scenarios
Test various routing scenarios to validate the configuration.

Test Chrome premium user (should go to v3):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s \
  -H "user-type: premium" \
  -H "user-agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/91.0.4472.124 Safari/537.36" \
  http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Test Firefox premium user (should go to v2):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s \
  -H "user-type: premium" \
  -H "user-agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64; rv:89.0) Gecko/20100101 Firefox/89.0" \
  http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Test user (should go to v3):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s \
  -H "test-user: true" \
  http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Test regular user (should go to v1):
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s \
  http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Task 3: Test Traffic Flow Between Services
Subtask 3.1: Implement Traffic Splitting
Configure traffic splitting to distribute load across multiple versions.

Create a traffic splitting virtual service:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-traffic-split
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
      weight: 50
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v2
      weight: 30
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v3
      weight: 20
EOF
This configuration splits traffic: 50% to v1, 30% to v2, and 20% to v3.

Test traffic distribution:
echo "Testing traffic distribution across versions..."
for i in {1..20}; do
  echo -n "Request $i: "
  kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s http://reviews:9080/reviews/1 | grep -o "reviews-v[123]" || echo "No version detected"
done
Subtask 3.2: Monitor Traffic Flow
Use Istio's observability features to monitor traffic flow.

Check proxy configuration:
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c istio-proxy -- pilot-agent request GET config_dump | grep -A 10 -B 10 "reviews"
View routing statistics:
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c istio-proxy -- pilot-agent request GET stats | grep reviews
Check cluster information:
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c istio-proxy -- pilot-agent request GET clusters | grep reviews
Subtask 3.3: Implement Fault Injection for Testing
Add fault injection to test service resilience.

Create a virtual service with fault injection:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-fault-injection
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - match:
    - headers:
        test-fault:
          exact: "delay"
    fault:
      delay:
        percentage:
          value: 100
        fixedDelay: 5s
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
  - match:
    - headers:
        test-fault:
          exact: "abort"
    fault:
      abort:
        percentage:
          value: 100
        httpStatus: 500
    route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
EOF
Test delay injection:
echo "Testing delay injection (should take ~5 seconds):"
time kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s -H "test-fault: delay" http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Test abort injection:
echo "Testing abort injection (should return 500 error):"
kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s -w "%{http_code}\n" -H "test-fault: abort" http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
Subtask 3.4: Implement Timeout and Retry Policies
Configure timeout and retry policies for better resilience.

Create a virtual service with timeout and retry:
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: reviews-resilience
  namespace: traffic-routing
spec:
  hosts:
  - reviews
  http:
  - route:
    - destination:
        host: reviews
        subset: v1
    timeout: 3s
    retries:
      attempts: 3
      perTryTimeout: 1s
      retryOn: gateway-error,connect-failure,refused-stream
EOF
Test the timeout policy:
echo "Testing timeout policy with a service that might be slow:"
time kubectl exec -n traffic-routing $SLEEP_POD -c sleep -- curl -s -m 10 http://reviews:9080/reviews/1
